"The bicycle, a simple yet revolutionary invention, has a history that spans centuries and continents. Its origins can be traced back to the early 19th century, when French inventor Pierre Michaux and his son built the first pedal-powered cycle, known as the 'velocipede.' These early models featured wooden frames and iron-rimmed wheels, and were propelled by foot pedals attached to the front wheel. Though rudimentary, the velocipede sparked a fascination with personal mobility.

In the 1860s, the 'safety bicycle' emerged, thanks to the ingenuity of John Kemp Starley in England. Starley's design introduced a chain-driven rear wheel and equal-sized wheels, vastly improving stability and comfort. This configuration became the standard for modern bicycles and laid the groundwork for mass production. The late 19th century saw the bicycle boom, with factories across Europe and America producing thousands of frames, and the sport of cycling gaining popularity.

The 20th century brought significant technological advances. Steel frames gave way to lighter alloys, and the introduction of pneumatic tires by John Boyd Dunlop in 1888 made rides smoother. The 1930s and 1940s introduced the first mass-produced aluminum and titanium frames, while the 1970s saw the rise of the 'bicycle boom' in the United States, fueled by environmental concerns and the counterculture movement. The 1990s introduced carbon fiber frames, offering unprecedented strength-to-weight ratios.

